{"id": "20500274", "revid": "10863197", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki?curid=20500274", "title": "Bovista plumbea", "text": "Bovista plumbea, commonly known as the tumbling puffball, tumbleball, or paltry puffball, is a small puffball mushroom. It is white when young and greyish in age. Easily confused with immature \"Bovista dermoxantha\", it is attached to the substrate by a tuft of mycelium. It is commonly found in Western Europe and California.\nSynonyms.\nObsolete synonyms for \"B. plumbea\" include:\nDescription.\nThe fruiting body of the sporocarp is broad, attached to the substrate by a tuft of mycelium, and spherical to slightly compressed. The exoperidium is white, becoming buff to pale-tan and minutely tomentose, and sometimes areolate. It eventually flakes away, or peels off in sheets, the latter occurring at maturation in hot, dry conditions. In contrast, the endoperidium membranes are lead-grey, with or without adhering fragments of exoperidium.\nSpores.\nThe spores are 5.0\u20136.5 x 4.0\u20135.5\u00a0\u03bcm, ovoid, thick-walled, and nearly smooth, with a central oil droplet, and a 7.5\u201311.5\u00a0\u03bcm pedicel. The capillitium is composed of individual elements, rather than interwoven, main branches thick-walled, flexuous, rapidly tapering, forking more or less dichotomously, ochre-colored in KOH.\nThe spores are released via a small apical pore. The gleba is white, turning dingy yellowish, olive-brown, finally dark-brown and firm-textured. However, the subgleba and sterile base are usually absent. Fruiting occurs throughout the mushroom season.\nDistribution and habitat.\nIt is commonly found in Western Europe and California.\nIt often lives in scattered to clustered in disturbed areas, especially in sparse grass.\nUses.\nThe young globes can be halved and cooked, but are bland and too insubstantial to be of much interest."}
